  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nWith a warm front over the area, several mid-level shortwaves\nwill move through the region along with a low pressure system\nthrough the Ohio Valley. This pattern will allow for several\nrounds of rain activity through Sunday evening. Overall, the\nthunder and severe weather potential is limited, as ongoing and\nexpected additional activity will keep instability down across\nthe region. However, there remains plenty of moisture in the\natmosphere, as forecast soundings indicated precipitable water\nvalues of 1.80-2.00\" across the area. Therefore, any rainfall\nhas the potential to be efficient. Any flooding concerns will be\nlimited to where rounds of rain can persist over the next 24-30\nhours. However, for our area, the setup is trending to allowing\nseveral gaps in the rain coverage at times and the better\nchances for flooding could be areas south and west.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBehind the weekend system, the pattern will shift to a more\nfall-like pattern starting on Monday with cool high pressure\nbuilding from the north and a cooler trough entering from the\nnorthwest. This will allow for temperatures to fall into the 60s\nfor much of the week and lows in the 40s and 50s. There could be\nsome potential for rain, mainly on Tuesday and Wednesday, as a\ntrough will remain over the Ohio Valley and could allow for some\nrain to build north into the area, as the high pressure system\nretreats slightly north. High pressure will build back in on\nThursday and try to dry out the forecast toward the end of the\nweek.",
